The date dimension — the universal dimension.

Almost every fact has a date, and almost every question has a time filter — this quarter, year over year, weekends only. So one dimension appears in virtually every warehouse and joins to nearly every fact: dim date, the universal dimension.

Its grain is one row per calendar day, and it's populated once, ahead of time, for years into the past and the future. Its surrogate key is a readable year-month-day integer — two-oh-two-six-oh-seven-two-four for the twenty-fourth of July, twenty twenty-six — so the key is both a fast integer join and human-legible. And around it sits a wealth of attributes: the full date, day of week, month name, quarter, year, week of year, is-weekend, is-holiday, fiscal year, fiscal quarter.

Now, you could just call a month or extract function at query time — so why store a whole table? Because a table holds what SQL cannot compute. First, the business calendar: is-holiday, for Spanish national and regional holidays, plus fiscal year and fiscal quarter. No date function knows your fiscal calendar, or which days are holidays. Second, consistent labels: everyone gets the same spelling of month name and quarter, so reports agree with each other. Third, rich, ready attributes: is-weekend, week of year, day and quarter names — so you filter and group without rewriting date logic in every query. And fourth, fast integer joins: the fact joins on a four-byte date key, not a date comparison.

And dim date is the archetype of two patterns we've just seen. It's the archetypal conformed dimension — shared by every fact. And it's the archetypal role-playing dimension — order date, ship date, and delivery date all point here. So build it richly, once, and the entire warehouse speaks the same calendar: one pre-computed row per day, a year-month-day key, and every calendar and fiscal attribute a report could ever ask for.
